From fd2d30ce5f95a2db4b582f44d40d13b0fb109218 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fountain5405 <Fountain5405@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 19:55:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/9] fcmps: fold both circuits in one rebuild, peeking the
 second z past the first proof

Why: the previous commit builds the circuits three times. Each build is the
full circuit construction, which costs time on every proof.

generalized-bulletproofs gains skip_verify_transcript (additive). It performs
exactly the reads and challenges verify performs for a statement of n
generators and c vector commitments, so the second proof's z can be peeked on
a clone right after the discarding build. Fcmp::verify now builds the
circuits twice (discard; fold both) instead of three times, and checks that
the real transcript after the first verify equals the skipped clone.

Safety:
- skip_verify_transcript is only used on a clone. The real transcript is
  still consumed by the unchanged verify.
- If the skip's read count ever differed from verify's for some n and c,
  the transcript comparison would fail and the proof would be rejected
  (InconsistentCircuitReplay): it fails closed.
- Verification only; public data.

Tests: generalized-bulletproofs' fuzz_test_arithmetic_circuit now also checks
that skip_verify_transcript ends at the same transcript position as verify,
for every statement it generates. The fcmps tests from the previous commit
cover the two-build path.

Measured at the node, on a 12-core Ryzen 9 3900X (16 GB RAM, SATA SSD), 128-input blocks 3,105,401-3,105,444:
wall 159 -> 140 s (-12%) and CPU 1,404 -> 1,226 s (-13%) against the
three-build version. Peak memory at the node is unchanged (+1%); per proof
the crate benchmark shows about +20% heap, since both folded accumulators are
live at once, and about -20% time.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
